2:02:20 PM [express] POST /api/ai/chat 200 in 99307ms :: {"response":"Okay, I am ready to provide a tailored weight loss diet plan for Mani. Here’s what I recommend:\n\n## 🌅 Breakfast\n**~450 kcal | 25g | 50g | 15g**\n*   Oats Porridge (made with skimmed milk): ½ cup cooked oatmeal (~150kcal, 5gP, 25gC, 2gF)\n*   Scrambled Egg Whites: 2 large (~70kcal, 12gP, 1gC, 5gF)\n*   Spinach: ¼ cup chopped (~10kcal, 1gP, 2gC, 0gF)\n*   Small portion of grilled paneer cubes ~100 g (~150kcal, 15gP, 5gC, 5gF)\n\n## 🍎 Mid-Morning Snack\n**~150 kcal | 10g| 15g | 5g**\n*   Apple slices with 1 tbsp almond butter (~150kcal, 5gP, 15gC, 5gF)\n\n## 🍽️ Lunch\n**~600 kcal | 35g | 70g | 20g**\n*   Chicken Breast Curry (skinless chicken breast - approx 120g) served with brown rice (½ cup cooked) (~400kcal, 35gP, 60gC, 10gF)\n*   Cucumber Salad : 1 medium cucumber sliced (~20kcal, 1gP, 5gC, 0gF)\n\n## 🌆 Evening Snack\n**~150 kcal | 5g | 20g | 8g**\n*   Greek Yogurt (plain, non-fat) – 1 container (~150kcal, 15gP, 10gC, 0gF)\n*   Few berries (~20kcal, 1gP, 5gC, 0gF)\n\n## 🌙 Dinner\n**~500 kcal | 30g | 40g | 20g**\n*   Fish (Salmon fillet - about 100g) baked with lemon juice and herbs (~250kcal, 25gP, 5gC, 15gF)\n*   Steamed Vegetables (broccoli, carrots, bell peppers) (~150kcal, 5gP, 20gC, 5gF)\n\n## ⚠️ Foods to Avoid\n*   Processed snacks like chips and cookies\n*   Sugary drinks such as soda and fruit juices\n*   Fried foods\n*   Excessive amounts of cheese and cream\n*   High sugar desserts\n\n## 💡 Why This Plan\nThe primary focus here is to reduce calories while ensuring sufficient protein to preserve lean body mass during fat loss. The increased fiber from whole grains helps promote satiety which will aid in reducing hunger cravings throughout the day. It also includes plenty of fruits and vegetables rich in vitamins and minerals essential for optimal health.\n\nSodium is normal, therefore there isn't anything special required regarding sodium intake. However, since he prefers Indian cuisine, I would suggest using minimal amount of salt.\n\nPotassium is within normal range, thus no need to adjust potassium intake further.\n\nCreatinine is slightly elevated suggesting mild renal impairment due to high protein consumption. To address this, the plan focuses on lower protein intakes compared to his usual diet by incorporating more plant-based sources of protein along with smaller portions of animal products. Hydration should be maintained through drinking ample fluids daily.\n\nUrea/BUN is normal indicating healthy kidneys function. Therefore, no adjustments are necessary related to urea/BUN level.\n\nHemoglobin/Iron is normal meaning Iron deficiency does not exist. Thus, no additional supplementation is recommended.\n\nVitamin D is assumed normal given lack of information otherwise. Supplementation can always occur if deficient.\n\nCholesterol/Lipids is presumed normal without cholesterol data available. Saturated fat content may require reduction depending upon lipid panel results obtained later.\n\nPhosphorus is normal, hence no restrictions are warranted.\n\nUric acid is normal, hence no changes are warranted.\n\nBlood Sugar/HbA1c is unknown, however considering age group and BMI, monitoring blood glucose regularly might benefit him especially post exercise sessions. A diabetic diet could become important down the road once diagnosis occurs.\n\n[MACROS: Protein 25%, Carbs 45%, Fat 30%]","followUpQuestions":[]}
