2:11:37 PM [express] GET /api/records/user/1 304 in 2ms :: [{"id":50,"userId":1,"symptom":"i am having diabetes","diagnosis":"Can you tell me how long you’ve had those symptoms?","fullConversation":[{"role":"assistant","text":"Hello Mani. I'm ready to help. How are you feeling today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having diabetis"},{"role":"assistant","text":"What kind of diabetes do you have?"},{"role":"user","text":"i dont know"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Have you been diagnosed by a healthcare professional?"},{"role":"user","text":"no"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Okay. What brings you in today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having diabetes"}],"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:17:13.517Z"},{"id":49,"userId":1,"symptom":"no","diagnosis":"Okay. What brings you in today?","fullConversation":[{"role":"assistant","text":"Hello Mani. I'm ready to help. How are you feeling today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having diabetis"},{"role":"assistant","text":"What kind of diabetes do you have?"},{"role":"user","text":"i dont know"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Have you been diagnosed by a healthcare professional?"},{"role":"user","text":"no"}],"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:16:59.944Z"},{"id":48,"userId":1,"symptom":"i dont know","diagnosis":"Have you been diagnosed by a healthcare professional?","fullConversation":[{"role":"assistant","text":"Hello Mani. I'm ready to help. How are you feeling today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having diabetis"},{"role":"assistant","text":"What kind of diabetes do you have?"},{"role":"user","text":"i dont know"}],"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:16:52.989Z"},{"id":47,"userId":1,"symptom":"i am having diabetis","diagnosis":"What kind of diabetes do you have?","fullConversation":[{"role":"assistant","text":"Hello Mani. I'm ready to help. How are you feeling today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having diabetis"}],"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:16:40.254Z"},{"id":46,"userId":1,"symptom":"i have high fever","diagnosis":"1.  What could this be?\n    *   Most Common: Viral infection such as influenza (flu) or mononucleosis (\"mono\"). These often cause fever, muscle aches, sweating, and fatigue. Less common possibilities include bacterial infections (like strep throat), less commonly COVID-19.\n\n2.  To which doctor should I refer?\n    *   General Physician: This is always the best starting point for assessing general health concerns. They can evaluate the overall picture and determine whether further referral is needed. After seeing the GP, depending on findings, consider consulting Infectious Disease Specialist if viral etiology suspected; Rheumatologist if musculoskeletal involvement significant; or Cardiologist if cardiac complications possible given age & risk factors.\n\n3.  Should I be worried?\n    *   NO - The symptoms sound relatively mild and consistent with typical flu or another common illness. However, close monitoring is advised until diagnosis confirmed by a physician.\n\n4.  Do I need to go to a hospital right now?\n    *   No. Symptoms align with uncomplicated febrile illnesses treatable in outpatient settings. Go to ER only if experiencing severe chest pain, difficulty breathing, confusion, seizures, persistent vomiting, inability to keep down fluids, stiff neck, rash spreading rapidly, or extreme lethargy.\n\n5.  How urgent is this?\n    *   See a doctor within 3 days. Monitor closely for worsening symptoms. Seek earlier evaluation sooner than three days if symptoms worsen significantly.\n\n6.  What can I do right now at home?\n    *   Rest completely. Stay hydrated by drinking plenty of water, broth, or electrolyte solutions. Over-the-counter acetaminophen (Tylenol) or ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin) can help reduce fever and relieve body aches *if appropriate*, considering no known contraindications. Avoid strenuous activity.\n\n7.  What should I avoid?\n    *   Avoid alcohol consumption while ill. Limit caffeine intake due to its diuretic effect potentially leading to dehydration. Avoid smoking.\n\n8.  What should I eat or drink?\n    *   Eat easily digestible foods like soup, toast, crackers, bananas, rice, applesauce – the BRAT diet. Small frequent meals are better tolerated. Ensure adequate fluid intake throughout the day.\n\n9.  Can I take any medicine on my own?\n    *   Acetaminophen (Tylenol): Maximum dose 1000mg every 4–6 hours, not more than 3 doses in 24 hours unless directed otherwise by a healthcare professional. Ibuprofen (Motrin, Advil): Maximum dose 400 mg every 4–6 hours, not more than 6 doses in 24 hours. Aspirin is generally avoided in children and adolescents due to Reye’s syndrome risk. Consult a doctor before using combination cold medicines containing decongestants or cough suppressants.\n    *   Disclaimer: Do not self-medicate. Please consult a General Physician before taking any medication.\n\n10. What symptoms should I watch out for?\n    *   Worsening shortness of breath or chest discomfort. Severe headache accompanied by stiff neck or altered mental status. Persistent dizziness or fainting spells. Seizures. Difficulty swallowing or speaking. Significant abdominal pain. Rash appearing suddenly. Signs of Dehydration (decreased urination, dark urine, dry mouth).\n\n11. Is this related to something I already have?\n    *   The provided information does not contain enough details regarding underlying medical conditions, past travels, allergies, or family history to definitively link the presented symptoms directly to pre-existing conditions. Further questioning would be required during examination to assess potential connections between existing conditions and presenting complaints. Consider previous vaccinations against Influenza or Pneumonia when evaluating possibility of vaccine failure.\n\n12. What will the doctor likely ask me?\n    *   Duration and onset of fever. Specific characteristics of the fever (e.g., constant, intermittent). Details about accompanying symptoms (muscle aches, sweats, chills, sore throat, cough, nausea, diarrhea, headache). Any recent exposures to sick individuals. Medications currently being taken. Allergies. Past medical history including vaccination records. Travel history. Family History of autoimmune disorders or chronic inflammatory diseases. Lifestyle habits (smoking, alcohol use, exercise routine).\n\n13. What tests might the doctor suggest?\n    *   Rapid Strep Test: Rule out streptococcal pharyngitis (strep throat). Rapid Flu test: Confirm presence of influenza virus. Complete blood count (CBC): Assess white blood cell count to differentiate between bacterial and viral infections. Chest X-ray: Evaluate for pneumonia especially if respiratory distress present. Urine Analysis: Check kidney function and rule out urinary tract infection. PCR testing: More sensitive detection of viruses like SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) or others.","fullConversation":{"type":"symptom_check","onset":"2-3 days ago","severity":"moderate","selectedRelated":["Sweating","Muscle Aches"]},"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:15:17.603Z"},{"id":45,"userId":1,"symptom":"i have diabetes","diagnosis":"Given your diabetic status, managing hyperglycemia becomes crucial during illness. Are you currently monitoring your blood sugar levels regularly, and if yes, what were they last checked?","fullConversation":[{"role":"assistant","text":"Hello Mani. I'm ready to help. How are you feeling today?"},{"role":"user","text":"i am having fever"},{"role":"assistant","text":"How long has this fever been present?"},{"role":"user","text":"from today"},{"role":"assistant","text":"What was the highest temperature that you recorded?"},{"role":"user","text":"it is 101 degrees fahrenheit"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Do you also experience chills along with the fever?"},{"role":"user","text":"yes"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Are there any other accompanying symptoms like cough, sore throat, body aches, headache, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or fatigue?"},{"role":"user","text":"only cough, sore throat"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Is the cough dry or productive (bringing up mucus)? And how severe would you rate your sore throat pain out of ten?"},{"role":"user","text":"it is dry, around 7"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Have you recently traveled anywhere or been exposed to anyone who might be sick? Also, can you tell me if you take any over-the-counter medication such as paracetamol or ibuprofen?"},{"role":"user","text":"no i didnt travel,"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Okay. Have you had a cold or flu in the past few weeks?"},{"role":"user","text":"no"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Thank you. Since you mentioned both cough and sore throat, could you please elaborate further on the nature of the cough – is it constant, intermittent, hacking, etc.?"},{"role":"user","text":"constant"},{"role":"assistant","text":"Alright. Given the persistent cough and sore throat without any recent travel exposure, we should consider possible causes ranging from viral infections like common cold or influenza to less common possibilities depending on duration and progression. We will need some additional information regarding the characteristics of the cough and sore throat to narrow down potential diagnoses. However, given no underlying medical condition reported so far, let us start by considering the most probable cause first which may include upper respiratory tract infection(URTI) due to virus. Let’s begin"},{"role":"user","text":"i have diabetes"}],"createdAt":"2026-03-19T11:13:00.490Z"},{"id":44,"userId":1,"symptom":"constant","diagnosis":"Alright. Given the persistent cough and sore throat without any recent travel exposure, we should consider possible causes ranging from viral infections like common cold or influenza to less common possibilities depending on duration and progression. We will need some additional information regarding the characteristics of the cough and sore throat to narrow down potential diagnoses. However, given no underlying medical condition reported so far, let us start by considering the most